1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
A simple word. Hello. But when a hand trembles, one intended tap can

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
become several. We want the keyboard to adapt to the person.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:15,000
An estimated 1.1 million Americans live with Parkinson’s, according to the Parkinson’s Foundation.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Tremor and stiffness can make fine motor tasks harder. Some people also experience

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,000
a quieter voice.

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:29,000
We are Atharv Sharma and Pranav Atreya, freshmen at Livingston High School in

7
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,000
New Jersey. This is TypeParkinson: an iPhone app and system keyboard built with

8
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Swift and SwiftUI.

9
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:43,000
First, short setup exercises measure where touches land. A two-dimensional Gaussian describes the

10
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:49,000
person’s offset and spread. The keyboard uses that personal touch model, and a

11
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:55,000
standard-versus-personal check lets the user compare their own typing.

12
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,500
Next, smart correction combines touch evidence with word likelihood. A dynamic-programming alignment considers

13
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:06,000
repeated touches, missing letters and swapped letters. Tje can become the; hellloo becomes

14
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:11,500
hello. A one-tap Undo keeps the person in control. These are tested examples,

15
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:17,000
not patient outcomes.

16
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:21,750
An expert-set naive Bayes model describes touch patterns; it does not diagnose Parkinson’s.

17
00:01:21,750 --> 00:01:26,500
Core Motion recordings use a windowed Fourier analysis to estimate shaking strength and

18
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:31,250
frequency. AVFoundation supports relative voice-volume practice and speaking typed messages. This five-hertz graphic

19
00:01:31,250 --> 00:01:36,000
and the sensor readings are illustrative samples.

20
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:41,333
The app includes a direct comparison screen. Here, demo data shows four errors

21
00:01:41,333 --> 00:01:46,667
versus one and six fixes versus two. These sample numbers explain the check.

22
00:01:46,667 --> 00:01:52,000
They are not a clinical study or a promise of improvement.

23
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:57,000
The engineering stays on the device. The app shares numerical settings through an

24
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:02,000
App Group with the custom keyboard extension. Here is the real Swift candidate-scoring

25
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:07,000
code. The current simulator run passed 67 automated tests across 13 suites. Hardware

26
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:12,000
and keyboard integration still need real-device validation.

27
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:16,250
The same app brings together a daily check, spiral and rhythm practice, a

28
00:02:16,250 --> 00:02:20,500
tremor meter, voice practice and Speak. Progress, medication logs and PDF or CSV

29
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:24,750
exports help a person bring observations to their care team. Displayed histories are

30
00:02:24,750 --> 00:02:29,000
sample data.

31
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,000
There is no TypeParkinson server. The keyboard stores numerical touch parameters, never typed

32
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:35,000
text. Speak can save phrases and recent messages locally. These measurements describe activity;

33
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:38,000
they do not diagnose disease.

34
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:41,333
TypeParkinson. A keyboard that learns your hands. Atharv Sharma and Pranav Atreya, Livingston

35
00:02:41,333 --> 00:02:44,667
High School, New Jersey. Congressional App Challenge 2026. AI assistance and open-source dependencies

36
00:02:44,667 --> 00:02:48,000
are documented with the project.
